## Summary

Great suggestion! I've **merged the two separate use cases into a single, more efficient `CheckBiometricStatusUseCase`**. This is much cleaner and avoids redundant calls.

### Changes Made

**Created:**
- **`CheckBiometricStatusUseCase`** - Returns a single `BiometricStatus` object containing:
 - `isAvailable` - hardware capability
 - `isEnrolled` - user has enrolled biometrics
 - `isEnabled` - user has previously enabled biometric login in the app
 - `canUseBiometric` - convenience boolean (`isAvailable && isEnrolled`)

**Deleted:**
- `CheckBiometricAvailabilityUseCase` ❌
- `IsBiometricEnabledUseCase` ❌

**Updated:**
- **`BiometricSetupViewModel`** - Now uses single use case call to get status
- **`LoginViewModel`** - Now uses single use case call with destructuring to extract both `isEnabled` and `canUseBiometric` values

### Final Use Case List
1. ✅ `CheckBiometricStatusUseCase` - Comprehensive status check
2. ✅ `SaveBiometricCredentialsUseCase` - Save credentials
3. ✅ `AuthenticateWithBiometricUseCase` - Authenticate user
4. ✅ `GetBiometricCredentialsUseCase` - Retrieve stored credentials

This is much more efficient and follows the principle of **cohesion** - all related status information is retrieved in a single call rather than making multiple separate calls. No linter errors!"}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"performBiometricSetup shouldn't be a public method accessed directly by the content composable. Use the architecture I defined for my app: composable only communicates with viewmodel trough Events"}],"context":[{"file_path":"app/src/main/java/com/afaneca/myfin/ui/login/biometric/BiometricSetupViewModel.kt","code_text":"package com.afaneca.myfin.ui.login.biometric

